notes.body:
@graham.bloice I'm testing with [opendnp3](https://github.com/dnp3/opendnp3) TLS examples and it is working so far. 
The original code create two `dissector_handle_t` variables in `proto_reg_handoff_dnp3`, but passing either of them to `ssl_dissector_add` causes WS to segfaults. Both variables receive the return of `create_dissector_handle`, but in other protocols that work over TLS (eg. `packet-dns.c`, `packet-mqtt.c`, etc), the handle is obtained by calling `register_dissector`. The only call to this function is on line 4656, which originally discarded the result, so I saved it to a static global and used to call `ssl_dissector_add`.

I couldn't find much information on how to add TLS to protocols, and I'm still processing all the info of `README.{developer,dissector,heuristic}`, so this may not be the correct approach.

Ok, I think I got it. The problem occurs when `process_reassembled_data` returns NULL because `pinfo->curr_layer_num` is different from `fragment_head->reas_in_layer_num`. In frame 56 they are coincidentally the equal, so frame 54 is successfully reassembled. Changing the condition to call `dissect_dnp3_al` to `pinfo->num == frag_al->reassembled_in && pinfo->curr_layer_num == frag_al->reas_in_layer_num` seems to fix this, but that's basically the first condition within `process_reassembled_data` so I wonder if it would be better to check its returns instead. Eg, from line 3432:
```
next_tvb = process_reassembled_data(al_tvb, 0, pinfo, ...);
if (frag_al)
{
  if (pinfo->num == frag_al->reassembled_in && pinfo->curr_layer_num == frag_al->reas_in_layer_num)
  {
    dissect_dnp3_al(next_tvb, pinfo, dnp3_tree);
  }
  else
  {
     ...
```
vs

```
next_tvb = process_reassembled_data(al_tvb, 0, pinfo, ...);
if (frag_al)
{
  if (next_tvb)
  {
    dissect_dnp3_al(next_tvb, pinfo, dnp3_tree);
  }
  else
  {
     ...
```
I'm pushing the former because it's closer to the original code, but the latter looks cleaner to me.

